new file mode 100644
index 0000000..6b0e1d2
--- /dev/null
+++ b/docs/deployment.md
@@ -0,0 +1,88 @@
+# Deploying Fizzy on one server
+
+This guide runs Fizzy on a single Linux server with [Kamal](https://kamal-deploy.org):
+
+- One Docker container runs the web app and its background jobs. Solid Queue runs inside Puma, so there is no separate jobs process.
+- Data lives in SQLite databases and Active Storage uploads, all under `/rails/storage`. That path is a Docker volume named `fizzy_storage`, so it survives deploys.
+- kamal-proxy serves your hostname over HTTPS with a Let's Encrypt certificate.
+
+The configuration is in `config/deploy.yml`. Secrets go in `.kamal/secrets`.
+
+## What you need
+
+- A Linux server you can SSH into as root (or change `ssh.user`), with ports 80 and 443 open.
+- A DNS record for your hostname pointing at that server. Let's Encrypt needs it before the first deploy.
+- An SMTP server Fizzy can send mail through. Sign-in codes are sent by email, so you can't sign in without one.
+- On your own machine: Docker with buildx (Kamal builds the image locally), and this repository's gems (`bundle install`).
+
+## 1. Configure
+
+Edit `config/deploy.yml` and change the values marked `CHANGE`:
+
+- `host`: your public hostname. It is also used as the SSH target.
+- `smtp_address` and `mail_from`: your mail server and the From address.
+- `builder.arch`: change to `arm64` if the server is ARM.
+
+Then create your secrets file from the template:
+
+```sh
+cp .kamal/secrets.example .kamal/secrets
+```
+
+Fill in `SECRET_KEY_BASE`. Add `SMTP_USERNAME` and `SMTP_PASSWORD` if your mail server requires login. Add the VAPID keys if you want browser push notifications. The template includes the commands that generate each value.
+
+`.kamal/secrets` is git-ignored. Don't commit it. Also don't paste the output of `bin/kamal config` anywhere public, because it includes the secrets.
+
+## 2. Deploy
+
+```sh
+bin/kamal setup
+```
+
+This installs Docker on the server, builds the image, and starts kamal-proxy. The image goes to a registry that Kamal runs on your machine and tunnels to the server over SSH, so you don't need a registry account. Then it boots Fizzy. On first boot the app creates its databases and runs migrations.
+
+Open `https://<your host>` and sign in with your email address.
+
+For later releases, run:
+
+```sh
+bin/kamal deploy
+```
+
+## Day to day
+
+```sh
+bin/kamal app logs -f                                        # follow the logs
+bin/kamal console                                            # Rails console in the running container
+bin/kamal app exec --reuse "bin/rails runner 'puts User.count'"   # any other command
+```
+
+## Data, backups, and removal
+
+Everything that must persist is in the `fizzy_storage` volume on the server:
+
+- the SQLite databases `production.sqlite3`, `production_cable.sqlite3`, `production_cache.sqlite3`, and `production_queue.sqlite3`
+- uploaded files, under `production/files`
+
+Deploys, container restarts, and `bin/kamal remove` leave the volume alone. Only removing the volume itself (`docker volume rm fizzy_storage`) deletes data.
+
+Fizzy keeps its SQLite databases in WAL mode, so copying the files while the app runs can give you an inconsistent copy. For a consistent copy of a database, use SQLite's online backup from inside the container. It writes the copy into the same volume:
+
+```sh
+bin/kamal app exec --reuse "sqlite3 /rails/storage/production.sqlite3 '.backup /rails/storage/production-backup.sqlite3'"
+```
+
+Then archive the volume with your usual server backup tooling, and delete the `-backup` copy once it's saved. Do the same for the other databases if you want them consistent with each other.
+
+## Things to know
+
+- During a deploy, the old and new containers can briefly run against the same databases. Keep migrations backward-compatible so the old version keeps working until the new one takes over.
+- This setup runs on one server. The databases are local SQLite files, so don't point a second web server at this volume.
+- Keep `SECRET_KEY_BASE` stable. Changing it signs everyone out. Changing the VAPID keys breaks existing push subscriptions.
+
+## Troubleshooting
+
+- **`Secret 'X' not found in .kamal/secrets`**: you haven't created `.kamal/secrets`, or it's missing a key from `.kamal/secrets.example`.
+- **Let's Encrypt fails**: DNS doesn't point at the server yet, or ports 80 and 443 are blocked.
+- **Sign-in email doesn't arrive**: check `bin/kamal app logs`, where mail errors appear. Verify `SMTP_ADDRESS`, `SMTP_PORT`, `SMTP_TLS`, and the credentials, and check that your mail server accepts mail from `mail_from`.
+- **Links in emails point at the wrong host**: `APP_FQDN` in `config/deploy.yml` must match `proxy.host`. The template sets both from `host`.
diff --git a/test/deploy/kamal_config_test.rb b/test/deploy/kamal_config_test.rb
new file mode 100644
index 0000000..ae4ff92
--- /dev/null
+++ b/test/deploy/kamal_config_test.rb
@@ -0,0 +1,52 @@
+require "test_helper"
+require "kamal"
+
+# The single-server deploy depends on a few properties that a config edit can easily break:
+# data on a volume that survives deploys, HTTPS from the proxy, background jobs running in the
+# web container, and secrets kept out of the image. These tests pin them.
+class KamalConfigTest < ActiveSupport::TestCase
+  DEPLOY_FILE = Rails.root.join("config/deploy.yml")
+  SECRETS_TEMPLATE = Rails.root.join(".kamal/secrets.example")
+
+  setup do
+    @deploy = YAML.safe_load(ERB.new(DEPLOY_FILE.read, trim_mode: "-").result)
+  end
+
+  test "Kamal accepts the deploy config" do
+    assert_nothing_raised { Kamal::Configuration.create_from(config_file: DEPLOY_FILE, version: "test") }
+  end
+
+  test "databases and uploads live on a named volume" do
+    assert_includes @deploy["volumes"], "fizzy_storage:/rails/storage"
+  end
+
+  test "the proxy serves HTTPS for the host the app uses in links" do
+    assert_equal true, @deploy.dig("proxy", "ssl")
+    assert_equal @deploy.dig("proxy", "host"), @deploy.dig("env", "clear", "APP_FQDN")
+  end
+
+  test "background jobs run inside the web container" do
+    assert_equal [ "web" ], @deploy["servers"].keys
+    assert_not_equal "false", @deploy.dig("env", "clear", "SOLID_QUEUE_IN_PUMA")
+  end
+
+  test "the secrets template lists every secret the deploy config uses" do
+    assert_empty Array(@deploy.dig("env", "secret")) - secret_keys_in(SECRETS_TEMPLATE)
+  end
+
+  test "the secrets template holds no real values" do
+    SECRETS_TEMPLATE.read.each_line.grep(/\A[A-Z0-9_]+=/) do |line|
+      key, value = line.chomp.split("=", 2)
+      assert_empty value.to_s.strip, "#{key} must be left blank in the template"
+    end
+  end
+
+  test "the image build excludes Kamal config and secrets" do
+    assert_includes Rails.root.join(".dockerignore").read.lines.map(&:strip), "/.kamal"
+  end
+
+  private
+    def secret_keys_in(path)
+      path.read.lines.filter_map { |line| line[/\A([A-Z0-9_]+)=/, 1] }
+    end
+end
